    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_f6cdaae04e3162ef8205f0f7da1a29a3\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39245720\",\n      \"title\": \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder - PubMed\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-08-09\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Contributed equally.\\nc Representative AlphaFold models, from 5 structural models each of X1 and CAP-Mac peptides with up to 20 recycles, predict selective interaction with human LRP6 domain E1 (E1 and E2: teal, E3 and E4: yellow, AAV peptides: purple).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11381518\",\n      \"title\": \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-09-08\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"We proceeded with direct capsid antibody detection and validated conditions with a panel of AAV capsids, including AAV9 as well as five engineered AAV9 variants with enhanced potency in the CNS of non-human primates (NHPs) after systemic administration (Table 1, Fig. 1d)20,21,23,28.\\nFive structure models were produced using a protocol with up to 20 recycles, and MSA generated with MMseqs2 (UniRef+Environmental)^97 and templates from PDB70. The structure models were ranked using a weighted combination of pTM and iPTM scores as described in^52. All structure visualizations in figures were prepared using PyMOL (www.pymol.org).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-52149-0?code=e0a1e855-a690-4f4b-8313-dbb4703d692d&error=cookies_not_supported\",\n      \"title\": \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder | Nature Communications\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-09-08\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"c Representative AlphaFold models, from 5 structural models each of X1 and CAP-Mac peptides with up to 20 recycles, predict selective interaction with human LRP6 domain E1 (E1 and E2: teal, E3 and E4: yellow, AAV peptides: purple).\\n```\\n        .\\\")</sup> to build models of LRP6-AAV interaction complexes, which predicted that the X1 and CAP-Mac VR-VIII peptides bind LRP6 YWTD domain 1 (Fig. [4c](/articles/s41467-024-52149-0#Fig4) ). While the cooperative folding of E1 and E2 complicates testing of individual domains <sup>[53](/articles/s41467-024-52149-0#ref-CR53 \\\"Liu, C.-C\\n```\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.01.05.574399v1\",\n      \"title\": \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain-barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-01-05\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"We proceeded with antibody direct capsid detection and validated conditions with a panel of AAV capsids, including AAV9 as well as five engineered AAV9 variants with enhanced potency in the CNS of non-human primates (NHPs) after systemic administration (AAV.CAP-B22^28, AAV.CAP-Mac^21, AAV-MaCPNS1^23, AAV-MaCPNS2^23, and AAV9-X1.1^20)(Table 1 &\\nFive structure models were produced using a protocol with up to 20 recycles, and MSA generated with MMseqs2 (UniRef+Environmental)^81 and templates from PDB70. The structure models were ranked using a weighted combination of pTM and iPTM scores as described in^50.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/scientific-contributions/Seongmin-Jang-2227246001\",\n      \"title\": \"Seongmin Jang's research works | California Institute of Technology and other places\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"c Representative AlphaFold models, from 5 structural models each of X1 and CAP-Mac peptides with up to 20 recycles, predict selective interaction with human LRP6 domain E1 (E1 and E2: teal, E3 and E4: yellow, AAV peptides: purple).\\nGenomic DNA and total RNA were extracted by using DNeasy Blood and Tissue Kit (Qiagen, #69504) and RNeasy Mini Kit (Qiagen, cat #74104), respectively. Adeno-associated virus (AAV) was produced in 293T cells according to previously published protocols (Jang et al., 2022; Huang et al., 2013) (Supplementary Methods).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.01.05.574399v1.full-text\",\n      \"title\": \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain-barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder | bioRxiv\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-01-01\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"We proceeded with antibody direct capsid detection and validated conditions with a panel of AAV capsids, including AAV9 as well as five engineered AAV9 variants with enhanced potency in the CNS of non-human primates (NHPs) after systemic administration (AAV.CAP-B22 28 , AAV.CAP-Mac 21 , AAV-MaCPNS1 23 , AAV-MaCPNS2 23 , and AAV9-X1.1 20 )( Table 1\\nAAVs were produced as previously described 77 . Briefly, HEK293 cells were triple transfected with capsid, genome, and helper plasmids. Media was exchanged the next day then collected and replaced two days after. At five days post-transfection, media and cells were collected and processed for AAV purification.\\nFive structure models were produced using a protocol with up to 20 recycles, and MSA generated with MMseqs2 (UniRef+Environmental) 81 and templates from PDB70. The structure models were ranked using a weighted combination of pTM and iPTM scores as described in 50 .\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.01.05.574399v1.full.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"[PDF] Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"improved vectors, as well as other therapeutic protein modalities, for specific targets 33,34 .\\nResults\\nHigh-throughput screening for AAV binding partners\\nTo screen AAV-binding proteins, we used Retrogenix cell microarrays 31,32 of the human membrane proteome\\nincluding AAV9 as well as five engineered AAV9 variants with enhanced potency in the CNS of non-human\\nprimates (NHPs) after systemic administration (AAV.CAP-B22 28 , AAV.CAP-Mac 21 , AAV-MaCPNS1 23 , AAV-\\nMaCPNS2 23 , and AAV9-X1.1 20 )(Table 1 & Figure 1d) 20,21,23,28 . Testing these capsids individually revealed that all\\nImages were analyzed in Zen Black 2.3 SP1 (Zeiss) and ImageJ.\\nAlphaFold structure modeling\\nThe complex structures of LRP6 ECD and AAV-X1 or AAV.CAP-Mac VR-VIII peptide were modeled using a\\ncloud-based implementation of AlphaFold-Multimer-v3 50 provided in ColabFold v2.3.5 80 . The input comprised\\nWe ran the Google Colaboratory notebook using an A100 SXM4 40GB GPU. Five structure\\nmodels were produced using a protocol with up to 20 recycles, and MSA generated with MMseqs2\\n(UniRef+Environmental) 81 and templates from PDB70. The structure models were ranked using a weighted\\ncombination of pTM and iPTM scores as described in 50 .\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/advances/pdfExtended/S2329-0501(25)00224-4\",\n      \"title\": \"The amazing AAV capsids: Into the structure-verse\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"which revealed large protrusions surrounding the three-fold symme-\\ntry axes and narrow channels at the five-fold axes. 45 In 2002 the\\nstructure of the AAV2 capsid was determined by X-ray crystallog-\\nraphy at 3 Å resolution, 46 marking the beginning of structural char-\\nacterization of AAV capsids. The AAV2 structure, as well as those of\\nassemble through 2-, 3-, and 5-fold symmetry interactions. 54 These\\ninteractions result in the formation of cylindrical channels by five\\nDE loops at the icosahedral 5-fold symmetry axes. The 5-fold chan-\\nnels connect the interior of the capsid to the external environment\\nFigure 3. Capsid structures of primate AAVs\\nsecondary structure matching, the VP3 models of AAV2 and AAV4\\nwere superposed, resulting in an overall C α RMSD of 1.0 Å. 66 While\\nthe capsid core showed nearly exact overlap of both structures’ coor-\\ndinates, significant positional differences were observed in the sur-\\nface loops. Nine regions of structural variability were identified be-\\ning sites of mouse monoclonal antibodies were mapped to the cap-\\nsids of AAV1, AAV2, AAV4, AAV5, AAV6, AAV8, AAV9, and\\nAAVrh.10 at resolutions ranging from ∼ 5 to 20 Å. 103–112 At these\\nresolutions, models of previously determined high-resolution capsid\\nand antibody structures were fitted into the lower-resolution maps to\\nonly about a quarter of the size of the Fab fragment of a standard\\nIgG and are used in affinity chromatography purification protocols,\\nenabling scalable \\\"Good Manufacturing Practice\\\" purification of\\nAAV vectors. The most commonly utilized nanobodies, AVB and\\nAAVX, enable the purification of a wide range of AAV capsids. 116 , 117\\nmany AAVs is KIAA0319L, also known as AAVR. 120 Its extracellular\\nportion contains five Ig-like polycystic kidney disease (PKD) do-\\nmains. Constructs comprising PKD1-5 or PKD1-2 have been com-\\nplexed with the capsids of AAV1, -2, -5, -9, AAVgo.1, and the engi-\\nneered capsid PHP.eB (see the capsid structures of engineered AAVs\\nCurrently, all high-resolution cryo-EM structures for capsid com-\\nplexes are determined using single-particle reconstructions, which\\ninvolve the structural averaging of a large number of homogeneous\\nparticles. However, the flexible nature of some proteins interacting\\nwith the AAV capsid using this technique results in them being aver-\\nand other cellular proteins that enhance or inhibit cell transduction\\nwill provide more comprehensive databases. These data can be lever-\\naged by ML- and AI-based technologies to generate reliable, AI-de-\\nsigned capsids. Recent breakthroughs in ML and AI have revolution-\\nized predictive structural modeling. AlphaFold’s structural\\ntherapy clinical trials.\\nAnother aspect of structural determination that will be increasingly\\naddressed with recent improvements in 3D reconstruction protocols\\nand higher computing power is deviations from the icosahedral sym-\\nmetry of the capsid. This will be particularly important for resolving\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.64898/2026.03.15.711945v1.full.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"[PDF] LRP6-Guided Engineering of AAV9 Variants with Enhanced Blood\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"These authors contributed equally.\\nand structural cross-species conservation among mice, NHPs and humans, represents one\\nsuch compelling target. 11,12 Herein, we describe our integrated approach combining LRP6-\\ntargeted rational design with a multi-tiered screening pipeline to develop novel AAV9\\nThese authors contributed equally.\\nrationally designed, LRP6-targeted library (~100 variants) in vitro using the human\\ncerebral microvascular endothelial cell line hCMEC/D3, a representative model of the\\nBBB endothelium. Cells were infected with GFP-expressing AAV variants at varying\\nThese authors contributed equally.\\nmultiplicities of infection (MOI: 2E3, 5E3, 1E4 and 5E4) for 48 hours. The five lead\\nvariants (QL9-1, QL9-3, QL9-21, QL9-22, and QL9-25) exhibited transduction\\nefficiencies consistently superior to the parental AAV9 and non-inferior to the benchmark\\ncapsid, AAV9-X1 (Figure 1A-D).\\nThese authors contributed equally.\\n\\\"efficacy-enhanced, toxicity-reduced\\\" profile of our LRP6-targeted variants represents a\\nhighly desirable outcome. 3,19 Mechanistically, this suggests that the rational mutations\\nintroduced might also altered AAV interactions with hepatocyte-specific attachment\\nfactors or receptors. 20\\nThese authors contributed equally.\\nAAV Vector Production and Purification\\nAll AAV vectors (including wild-type AAV9, benchmark AAV9-X1, and engineered\\nvariants QL9-21, QL9-22, QL9-25 et al.) were produced using the standard triple-plasmid\\ntransfection method in suspension HEK293 cells (Thermo Fisher Scientific, VPCs2.0,\\nA52021).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11436092\",\n      \"title\": \"Correction: Lopez-Gordo et al. Natural Adeno-Associated Virus Serotypes and Engineered Adeno-Associated Virus Capsid Variants: Tropism Differences and Mechanistic Insights. Viruses 2024, 16, 442\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-08-28\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Recently, a study confirmed the lack of binding of AAV.CAP-Mac, AAV-MaCPNS1, AAV-MaCPNS2, and AAV9-X1.1 to Ly6A [315].”\\n“Low-density-lipoprotein-receptor-related-protein 6 (LRP6) was identified as a receptor for AAV9-X1.1, AAV.CAP-Mac, and AAV-BI30.\\nLRP6 is a co-receptor of the canonical Wnt signaling pathway, which is present in various tissues [327].\\nThrough pull-down and SPR assays, binding and functional assays on cells, as well as competition assays and modeling with AlphaFold-Multimer [328], the authors confirmed these interactions through the extracellular YWTD domains 1 and 2 (E1E2) [329] of LRP6, with variable sensitivities, and described the inserted peptide as being sufficient to\\n“Nevertheless, cargo engineering can also help in addressing this issue by using liver-specific transcriptional regulatory elements to reduce transgene expression (e.g., miRNA-122 [340], used in AAV9-X1 [252]).”,\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_ea39878bfffeVgc2zisEYeoaLE\"\n}"
