    "role": "tool",
    "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a6472e083cf66319",
    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_50be33c2c3492751dbec29b6ee7a3b09\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://mirrors.ibiblio.org/CRAN/web/packages/appraise/index.html\",\n      \"title\": \"CRAN: Package appraise\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2026-02-09\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The package models study-level effect estimates by explicitly accounting for multiple sources of bias through prior distributions and propagates uncertainty using posterior simulation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2310.11716v1\",\n      \"title\": \"Reflection-Tuning:Data Recycling Improves LLM Instruction-Tuning\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Specifically, our recycled WizardLM 7B model achieves the highest win rate among other open-source 7B models in the Alpaca-Eval leaderboard. Moreover, Our recycled Alpaca achieves a win rate of $88.75%$ and our recycled WizardLM achieves a win rate of $81.25%$ on the Vicuna [ 7 ] test set with the same number of training data and model size.\\ninstruction-response pairs. Given the initial base dataset, we are motivated to generate a high-quality version of each data point with an oracle model, ChatGPT for instance. However, a common problem with using LLMs as judges is the failure to obtain diverse results.\\nWe name the whole above process as a recycling process, which greatly improves the quality of the previous dataset. Then the raw model $f_{\\\\theta}$ will be trained on the newly generated recycled dataset, and the newly generated models are notated as “Recycled Models”, eg. Recycled Alpaca.\\nThe Adam optimizer [ 18 ] , with a $2\\\\times 10^{-5}$ learning rate and a batch size of $128$ , steers the training across three epochs with a max length of $2048$ . The warmup rate is set to $0.03$ .\\nAs delineated in [ 5 ] , models subjected to evaluation are prompted to generate outputs for each instruction in the test corpus. Subsequent to this, an API-driven model, be it GPT4 or ChatGPT, allocates a score to each response. A model’s superiority on this dataset hinges on its endorsement by the adjudicating model.\\nThe adjudication phase entails rating each model-generated response on a scale spanning from $1$ to $10$ , with scores encapsulating facets such as pertinence and precision.\\nEach bar represents a comparison between our recycled model and the other model. The red parts represent the number of wins and the green parts represent the number of loses. GPT4 is utilized as the judge.\\nTable 2 showcases the performance comparison on the Huggingface Open LLM Leaderboard with some related models. With our Recycle mechanism, our models achieve better average performances across these four representative benchmarks and our results are comparable to llama-2-7b-chat, which is elaborately fine-tuned with extra RLHF.\\n* Recycled WizardLM 7B | 66.9 | 518.7 | 10.0 | 3.2 | 2.5 | 0.73 | 0.81\\nTable 3: The comparison of performance for various models with different metrics. “Ins. len” and “Res. len” represent the average token length of the instructions and responses. “Ins. ppl” represents the average perplexity of instructions. “Res. ppl 1” and “Res.\\nWe further train a Recycled Alpaca in the 13B version to further validate the efficacy of our method. With only $52$ k recycled alpaca data being used for instruction-tuning, our Recycled Alpaca 13B reaches the win rate of $83.42%$ in the Alpaca Eval leaderboard and reaches an average score of $58.93%$ on Huggingface Open LLM leaderboard.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/google/git-appraise\",\n      \"title\": \"git-appraise module - github.com/google/git-appraise - Go Packages\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"A complete introduction to building software with Go\\n* Standard library\\nReference documentation for Go's standard library\\n* Release Notes\\nLearn what's new in each Go release\\n* API\\nReference documentation for the pkg.go.dev API\\n* Packages\\n* Community submenu dropdown icon\\n* Recorded Talks\\nVideos from prior events\\n* Meetups\\nThe code review data is stored in git-notes , using the formats described below. Each item stored is written as a single line of JSON, and is written with at most one such item per line. This allows the git notes to be automatically merged using the \\\"cat_sort_uniq\\\" strategy.\\nRobot comments are comments generated by static analysis tools. These are stored in the \\\"refs/notes/devtools/analyses\\\" ref, and annotate the revision. They must conform to the analysis schema .\\nReview Comments\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.coursehero.com/file/44152958/Acquire-Appraise-Aggregate-Report-Instructionspdf\",\n      \"title\": \"Acquire Appraise Aggregate Report Instructions.pdf - EBDM Report 2 - Acquire  Appraise  and Aggregate Overview Start by selecting one to the questions | Course Hero\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2019-07-30\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"457027888_Untitled_document_(4)_6808001645749970 (1).docx EBDM 2: Acquire, Appraise, & Aggregate ● EBDM Report 2 - Acquire, Appraise, and Aggrega... SOCIOLOGY SCE363 Univ\\nSee more\\nWhat is a pay-with-competition (Match) policy, a lead pay-level policy, and a lag pay-level policy. Q&A\\nDiscuss the accounting for uncollectible accounts - assum\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_ea39878bfffeVgc2zisEYeoaLE\"\n}"
