    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_b0cec31f4e4ffdd47415af4c13f280fa\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.clover.caltech.edu/aav\",\n      \"title\": \"AAV Vector Engineering\\n  \\n\\n     - \\n    \\n    CLOVER Center\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"* Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder. Shay T.F., Jang S., Brittain T.J., Chen X., Walker B., Tebbutt C., Fan Y., Wolfe D.A., Arokiaraj C.M., Sullivan E.E., Ding X., Wang T.Y., Lei Y., Chuapoco M.R., Chou T.F., Gradinaru V.\\nShay T.F., Sullivan E.E., Ding X., Chen X., Ravindra Kumar S., Goertsen D., Brown D., Crosby A., Vielmetter J., Borsos M., Wolfe D.A., Lam A.W., Gradinaru V. Science Advances , 2023; 10.1126/sciadv.adg6618. [ Article | Caltech News Release ]\\n* Spatial transcriptomics for profiling the tropism of viral vectors in tissues .\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://neuroscience.caltech.edu/documents/21479/Sullivan_Abstract_r2022.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"Presenter: Erin Sullivan, Gradinaru lab Title: Identifying Receptors for Enhanced\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Presenter: Erin Sullivan, Gradinaru lab\\nTitle: Identifying Receptors for Enhanced Blood-Brain Barrier Crossing by Engineered Adeno-\\nAssociated Viruses\\nAuthor(s): Timothy F. Miles, Erin E. Sullivan, Sripriya Ravindra Kumar, David Brown, Xinhong\\nChen, Jost Vielmetter, Mate Borsos, Annie W. Lam, Viviana Gradinaru\\nAbstract: Efficient transcytosis of the blood brain barrier (BBB) is a key bottleneck in the\\ndevelopment of molecules to study and treat the brain, particularly biologics requiring active\\ntransport. Our work on adeno-associated virus (AAV) engineering has yielded diverse capsid\\nvariants with enhanced central nervous system (CNS) infectivity upon systemic delivery in mice,\\nmarmosets, and macaques. However, the directed evolution methods by which we identified\\nthese capsids do not provide much insight into the mechanisms by which enhanced tropisms\\nare conferred, complicating the application of engineered AAV both across model organisms\\nand in potential human gene therapies. This dilemma is perhaps best illustrated by AAV-\\nPHP.eB, whose enhanced CNS tropism was found to be restricted to mouse strains expressing\\nmembrane-localized Ly6a after unsuccessful application in non-human primates and select\\nmouse strains. Here, we used surface plasmon resonance to assemble a panel of previously\\nidentified engineered AAVs with enhanced CNS tropism that do not interact with Ly6a.\\nAdministration of these capsids to the genetically diverse 129S1/SvlmJ, CBA/J, DBA/2J, and\\nNOD/ShiLtJ mouse strains showed enhanced CNS infectivity patterns consistent with non-Ly6a\\nmechanisms for BBB transcytosis. We developed a cell culture screen of candidate membrane\\nproteins selected based on their single-cell RNA sequencing profiles in C57BL/6J mouse cortex\\nand tested a diverse panel of engineered AAVs. Using this screen, we identified a potential\\nnovel role for Ly6c1 as a molecular receptor in the BBB that enables enhanced CNS infectivity\\nby multiple engineered AAVs.\\nThis receptor complements previously identified Ly6a-interacting\\nAAVs as research tools in diverse mouse strains.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.umassmed.edu/news/articles/archives/2022/05/umass-chan-research-explores-differences-in-aav-vector-manufacture-shows-clear-winner-for-method\",\n      \"title\": \"UMass Chan research explores differences in AAV vector manufacture, shows clear winner for method\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2022-05-20\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"In the past two decades, AAV vector manufacturing has made remarkable advancements to meet large-scale production demands for preclinical and clinical trials.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2665945X23000141\",\n      \"title\": \"Advances in AAV technology for delivering genetically ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Advances in AAV technology for delivering genetically ...\\nby LJ Campos · 2023 · Cited by 51 — AAV-CAP-Mac was found to primarily transduce primate neurons. AAVs, including AAV-PHP.eB. The GPI-linked protein LY6A drives AAV-PHP.B transport across the\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://thesis.caltech.edu/13752\",\n      \"title\": \"Engineering Vectors for Non-Invasive Gene Delivery to the Central Nervous System using Multiplexed-CREATE — CaltechTHESIS\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Author: Ravindra Kumar, Sripriya\\nYear: 2020\\nDegree: Dissertation (Ph.D.)\\nAdvisor: Gradinaru, Viviana\\nCommittee Members: Guttman, Mitchell; Lois, Carlos; Voorhees, Rebecca M.; Gradinaru, Viviana\\nOption: Biology\\nDOI: 10.7907/sgez-zn27\\nAbstract\\nViruses are widely modified and used as gene delivery vectors for various applications in science and therapeutics. To this end, my thesis focuses on modifying the recombinant adeno-associated viral (rAAV) vectors that are identified as a safer choice for cargo delivery compared to other known viral vectors.\\nThey are widely used in the scientific communities, have seen promising outcomes in gene therapy clinical trials, and as of today have three products approved to use in humans.\\nGradinaru lab addresses the issue by using a directed evolution approach called CREATE, Cre recombination-based AAV targeted evolution, to identify AAV-PHP.B and AAV-PHP.eB capsids, which broadly transduce the CNS (Deverman et al, 2016; Chan et al, 2017).\\nSelections across brain endothelial cells, neurons, and astrocytes yielded several AAV-PHP.B-like variants that broadly transduce the CNS, AAV-PHP.V variants that can efficiently transduce the vascular cells forming the BBB, a AAV-PHP.N variant that transduces neurons with greater specificity, and AAV-PHP.C variants that cross the BBB without\\nmurine strain specificity across tested strains. The AAV-PHP.C variants have different amino acid motifs compared to the AAV-PHP.Bs that have been previously shown to have limited CNS transduction across some mouse strains due to its interaction with the strain specific host cell surface receptor, ly6a, a homolog of which is not found in humans.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.genecopoeia.com/publications-by-technology-area/aav-products-and-services-publications\",\n      \"title\": \"AAV Products and Services Publications | GeneCopoeia™\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2021-09-07\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Science Advances doi: 10.1126/sciadv.abj8658 [ AAVPrime™ AAV particles expressing FOXO1, shRNA targeting FOXO1 ]\\n* Song, M-Y., et al. (2022. Sirt6 reprograms myofibers to oxidative type through CREB-dependent Sox6 suppression . Nature Communications doi: 10.1038/s41467-022-29472-5 [ AAVPrime™ AAV particles expressing Sirt6, shRNA targeting Sirt6 ]\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6855452\",\n      \"title\": \"LY6A, a novel cellular receptor for AAV-PHP.B capsids - PMC\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"LY6A, a novel cellular receptor for AAV-PHP.B capsids - PMC\\nby Q Huang · 2019 · Cited by 305 — eB-LY6A interactions are influenced by galactose levels. As expected, AAV9 and AAV-PHP.eB similarly bound and transduced Lec2 cells more efficiently than Lec8\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.broadinstitute.org/publications/broad625921\",\n      \"title\": \"LY6A, a novel cellular receptor for AAV-PHP.B capsids.\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"LY6A, a novel cellular receptor for AAV-PHP.B capsids.\\nThe engineered AAV-PHP.B family of adeno-associated virus efficiently delivers genes throughout the mouse central nervous system. To guide their application\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/molecular-therapy/fulltext/S1525-0016(19)30054-1\",\n      \"title\": \"The GPI-Linked Protein LY6A Drives AAV-PHP.B Transport ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The GPI-Linked Protein LY6A Drives AAV-PHP.B Transport ...\\nby J Hordeaux · 2019 · Cited by 282 — We studied this variant to determine whether LY6A remained the primary determinant of BBB permeability. Following i.v. injection of AAV-PHP.eB, we observed CNS\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/advances/fulltext/S2329-0501(22)00103-6\",\n      \"title\": \"Structural basis of receptor usage by the engineered ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Structural basis of receptor usage by the engineered ...\\nby S Jang · 2022 · Cited by 40 — Our results suggest that PHP.eB's altered tropism may arise from both an additional interaction with LY6A and weakening of its AAVR interaction. Changing the\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_ea39878bfffeVgc2zisEYeoaLE\"\n}"
