    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_51924aa32a676dae2f2ec2c4a7c7d730\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11381518\",\n      \"title\": \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-09-08\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder\\n* DOI: 10.1038/s41467-024-52149-0\\n* PMID: 39245720\\n* PMCID: PMC11381518\\nFinally, we functionally validate LRP6 capsid interactions in vivo in mice, in the human BBB via primary cell culture, and beyond the BBB using human pluripotent stem cell (hPSC)-derived neurons.\\nmodifications identified via mouse selections that potently targets brain endothelial cells in rodents and CNS neurons in macaques after intravenous injection^20.\\nWe then tested IL3 from different species, finding that AAV9 binds to human and macaque IL3 (83% amino acid [AA] identity) but not marmoset or mouse IL3 (69% and 27% AA identity with human, respectively) (Fig. 2c), suggesting a binding site divergence between new and old world monkeys.\\nValidation of AAV binding interaction with LRP6\\nWe next assessed the validated AAV interactors for their potential to explain the enhanced brain tropisms of the engineered capsids. Sorting the screen hits by their expression level in endothelial cells of the human BBB^47 spotlighted a specific interaction of LRP6 with AAV9-X1.1 (Fig. 4a).\\nThis capsid displays enhanced brain endothelial-specific tropism in mice that shifts to enhanced neuronal tropism in macaque (Table 1)^20.\\nThe high degree of LRP6 sequence conservation across species (98% and 99.5% AA identity between human LRP6 and mouse or macaque LRP6, respectively) aligns with AAV9-X1.1’s enhanced tropism compared to AAV9 in rodents and primates.\\nTo confirm that LRP6 interaction is mediating the previously-characterized enhanced in vivo brain potency of AAV9-X1.1 in primates^20, we tested the vector on macaque and human primary brain microvascular endothelial cells (PBMECs) in culture (Fig. 6a, b).\\nHuman, macaque, marmoset, mouse and chimeric interleukin 3 (human IL3: AA 1-152, macaque IL3: AA 1-144, marmoset IL3: AA 1-143, mouse IL3: AA 1-166, chimeras: AA1-152) triple tagged with human IgG1 Fc-Myc-6xHis, human and mouse GP2 (hGP2: AA 1-518, mGP2: AA 1-515) triple tagged with human IgG1 Fc-Myc-6xHis, and human and mouse DKK3 (hDKK3: AA\\nHuman brain microvascular endothelial cells (ScienCell Research Laboratories, cat# 1000) and cynomolgus macaque primary brain microvascular endothelial cells (CellBiologics, cat# MK-6023) were cultured as per the vendors’ instructions.\\nThe input comprised two sequences: surface-exposed residues in VR-VIII of AAV-X1 (587-AQGNNTRSVAQAQTG-594) or AAV-CAP-Mac (587-AQLNTTKPIAQAQTG-594) and the extracellular domain of human LRP6 (UniProt entry O75581, residues 20-1370). We ran the Google Colaboratory notebook using an A100 SXM4 40GB GPU.\\nReporting summary\\nFurther information on research design is available in the Nature Portfolio Reporting Summary linked to this article.\\nSupplementary information\\nThe online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1038/s41467-024-52149-0.\\nAcknowledgements\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-52149-0\",\n      \"title\": \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder | Nature Communications\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-09-08\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"```\\n         (2022).\\\")</sup> , and (5) AAV9-X1.1, an AAV9 variant containing both VR-IV and VR-VIII modifications identified via mouse selections that potently targets brain endothelial cells in rodents and CNS neurons in macaques after intravenous injection <sup>[20](/articles/s41467-024-52149-0#ref-CR20 \\\"Chen, X. et al. Functional gene delive\\n```\\nWe next assessed the validated AAV interactors for their potential to explain the enhanced brain tropisms of the engineered capsids. Sorting the screen hits by their expression level in endothelial cells of the human BBB 47 spotlighted a specific interaction of LRP6 with AAV9-X1.1 (Fig. 4a ).\\nThis capsid displays enhanced brain endothelial-specific tropism in mice that shifts to enhanced neuronal tropism in macaque (Table 1 ) 20 .\\n4: Primate brain-enhanced AAVs gain interaction with LRP6.\\nFull size image\\na Left: Arraying AAV capsid-specific hits by human brain endothelial cell expression levels reveals highly-conserved LRP6 as a potential receptor for BBB crossing. Right: the LRP6 extracellular domain contains 4 YWTD domains (E1-E4).\\n(2023).\\\")</sup> (Table [1](/articles/s41467-024-52149-0#Tab1) ), which was engineered in marmosets and has enhanced endothelial tropism compared to AAV9 in marmosets as well as enhanced neuronal tropism in macaque. Therefore, we also tested CAP-Mac by SPR for interaction with the human LRP6 extracellular domain (Fig.\\n[4b](/articles/s41467-024-52149-0#Fig4) ). As with IL3, we utilized avidity to ensure that weak yet functionally important interactions were captured. Both AAV9-X1.1 and CAP-Mac strongly bind human LRP6-Fc, unlike their parent capsid, AAV9, with a sub-nM apparent affinity.\\n```\\n        .\\\")</sup> to build models of LRP6-AAV interaction complexes, which predicted that the X1 and CAP-Mac VR-VIII peptides bind LRP6 YWTD domain 1 (Fig. [4c](/articles/s41467-024-52149-0#Fig4) ). While the cooperative folding of E1 and E2 complicates testing of individual domains <sup>[53](/articles/s41467-024-52149-0#ref-CR53 \\\"Liu, C.-C\\n```\\n```\\n#Fig4) ). We also tested AAV-BI30, another engineered capsid with specific tropism for the mouse brain endothelium <sup>[54](/articles/s41467-024-52149-0#ref-CR54 \\\"Krolak, T. et al. A high-efficiency AAV for endothelial cell transduction throughout the central nervous system. Nat. Cardiovasc Res 1, 389–400 (2022).\\\")</sup> (Table [1](/articl\\n```\\nTo confirm that LRP6 interaction is mediating the previously-characterized enhanced in vivo brain potency of AAV9-X1.1 in primates 20 , we tested the vector on macaque and human primary brain microvascular endothelial cells (PBMECs) in culture (Fig. 6 a, b ).\\nAAV9-X1.1 has enhanced potency in ( a ) macaque and ( b ) human primary brain microvascular endothelial cell culture (black data points), which decreases to AAV9 levels with Mesd inhibition of LRP6 (blue data points). 4 biological replicates were performed and quantified for each condition. Bars indicate the mean value.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-52149-0.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood ...\\nHere, we adapt an unbiased human cell microarray platform to determine the extracellular and cell surface interactomes of natural and engineered AAVs.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://artefacts-discovery.researcher.life/full_text/DA-2/9e/9e771ce8e71d393bb1f9b4b132e34340/full_text/245cec046efad117fb60872132bb0903.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-09-08\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Article https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-52149-0\\nHuman cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder\\nSorting the screen hits by their expression level in endothelial cells of the human BBB 47 spotlighted a specific interaction of LRP6 with AAV9-X1.1 (Fig. 4a). This capsid displays enhanced brain endothelial-specific tropism in mice that shifts to enhanced neuronal tropism in macaque (Table 1) 20 .\\nThe high degree of LRP6 sequence conservation across species (98% and 99.5% AA identity between human LRP6 and mouse or macaque LRP6, respectively) aligns with AAV9-X1.1’s enhanced tropism compared to AAV9 in rodents and primates.\\nAAV9-X1.1 DGAATKN AQGNNTRSVAQ Mouse CNS (endothelial) CNS (weak neuronal) CNS (neuronal) 20\\nAAV-BI30 AQNNSTRGGAQ Mouse CNS (endothelial) 54\\nArticle https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-52149-0\\nNature Communications| (2024) 15:7853 4\\ninteractions were captured.\\nAAV9 (parent of vectors below) DPP4 DKK3, IL3 IL3\\nAAV.CAP-B22 LY6A, FAM234A, DPP4 DKK3, IL3 LY6A, FAM234A\\nAAV-MaCPNS1 DPP4 DKK3, IL3\\nAAV-MaCPNS2 DPP4 DKK3, IL3\\nAAV.CAP-Mac DPP4, GP2 DKK3, IL3 LRP6, GP2\\nAAV9-X1.1 DPP4, GP2, LRP6, ANPEP DKK3, IL3, CSF2, EPYC LRP6, GP2\\nArticle https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-52149-0\\nTo confirm that LRP6 interaction is mediating the previously- characterized enhanced in vivo brain potency of AAV9-X1.1 in primates 20 , we tested the vector on macaque and human primary brain microvascular endothelial cells (PBMECs) in culture (Fig. 6a, b).\\nArticle https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-52149-0\\nNature Communications| (2024) 15:7853 7\\nexperiments (Supplementary Fig. 8a) showing that the X1 peptide is necessary and sufficient for LRP6 interaction, and thus BBB transcytosis.\\nUntreated\\nMacaque Primary Brain Microvascular Endothelial Cells\\nMesd Inhibitor Untreated\\nHuman Primary Brain Microvascular Endothelial Cells\\nAAV9\\n0\\n10\\n20\\n30\\n40\\nAAV9-X1.1\\nPercent transduced\\nAAV9-X1.1 AAV9 b\\nUntreated Mesd\\nMOI: 5 x 10 4 MOI: 5 x 10 4\\nAAV9-X1.1\\nMesd Inhibitor\\nAAV9\\nc\\nUntreated Untreated Mesd Inhibitor\\nAAV9 AAV9-X1.1\\n0\\n20\\n40\\n60\\n6 | LRP6 enhances engineered AAV potency in primate BBB and neuronal cell culture. AAV9-X1.1 has enhanced potency in (a) macaque and (b) human pri- mary brain microvascular endothelial cell culture (black data points), which decreases to AAV9 levels with Mesd inhibition of LRP6 (blue data points).\\nArticle https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-52149-0\\nNature Communications| (2024) 15:7853 9\\nThe high degree of sequence conservation of LRP6 (98% AA identity between mouse and human 61 ) helps explain the broad con- servation across species of enhanced tropism by AAV capsids targeting this receptor.\\nPrimary cell culture potency assay Human brain microvascular endothelial cells (ScienCell Research Laboratories, cat# 1000) and cynomolgus macaque primary brain microvascular endothelial cells (CellBiologics, cat# MK-6023) were cultured as per the vendors’ instructions.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.64898/2026.03.15.711945v1.full-text\",\n      \"title\": \"LRP6-Guided Engineering of AAV9 Variants with ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"LRP6-Guided Engineering of AAV9 Variants with ...\\nMar 18, 2026 — BBB Endothelial Cell Transduction: Human cerebral microvascular endothelial cells (hCMEC/D3, ATCC) were seeded in 24-well plates at 1×10 cells/\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.01.05.574399v1.full.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"[PDF] Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Validation of AAV binding interaction with low-density-lipoprotein-receptor-related-protein 6.\\nReturning to the identified AAV capsid-binding interactions, we then assessed the validated AAV\\ninteractors for their potential to explain the enhanced brain tropisms of the engineered capsids. Sorting the\\nscreen hits by their expression level in endothelial cells of the human BBB 45 spotlighted a specific interaction of\\nlow-density-lipoprotein-receptor-related-protein 6 (LRP6) with AAV9-X1.1 (Figure 3a). This capsid displays an\\nenhanced brain endothelial-specific tropism in mice that shifts to an enhanced neuronal tropism in macaque\\n(Table 1) 20 .\\nIn Lrp6 knockout conditions, decreased AAV9-X1.1 liver transduction was also observed (Figure\\n4b and c).\\nTo confirm that LRP6 interaction is mediating the brain potency of AAV9-X1.1 in primates, we tested the\\nvector on macaque and human primary brain microvascular endothelial cells (PBMECs) in culture (Figure 4d\\nand 4e).\\nof cell microarray screening to identify receptors for natural and engineered AAVs. We identify LRP6 as a novel\\nand highly conserved target for blood-brain barrier transcytosis by AAV9-X1.1, a potent engineered capsid for\\nprimate neurons 20 and human IL3 as an interaction partner for AAV9. These findings offer the prospect of\\nbinds to human and macaque (83% AA identity) but not marmoset or mouse IL3 (69% and 27% AA identity,\\nrespectively). It is possible that this species-dependent interaction could contribute to a disconnect between\\nrodent and primate AAV safety profiles 64–67 , especially in neurodegeneration contexts.\\nPrimate brain-enhanced AAVs gain interaction with LRP6 . a , Arraying AAV capsid-specific hits by\\nhuman brain endothelial cell expression levels reveals highly-conserved LRP6 as a potential receptor for BBB\\ncrossing. b , SPR confirms that the engineered capsids AAV9-X1.1 and CAP-Mac gained direct binding\\ninteractions with human LRP6.\\nand e, human primary brain microvascular endothelial cell culture, which decreases to AAV9 levels with Mesd\\ninhibition of LRP6. Bars indicate the mean value.\\navailable under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license .\\nin human primary brain microvascular endothelial cell culture, which decreases to AAV1 levels with Mesd\\ninhibition of LRP6. Bars indicate the mean value.\\navailable under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license .\\n(which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity.\\nIdentified cross-links were visualized\\nusing xiSPEC 79 .\\nPrimary cell culture potency assay\\nHuman brain microvascular endothelial cells (ScienCell Research Laboratories, cat# 1000) and cynomolgus\\nmonkey primary brain microvascular endothelial cells (CellBiologics, cat# MK-6023) were cultured as per the\\ninstructions provided by the vendor.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39245720\",\n      \"title\": \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder - PubMed\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-08-09\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"doi: 10.1038/s41467-024-52149-0.\\nHuman cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder\\nAffiliations Expand\\nAffiliations\\nContributed equally.\\n* PMID: 39245720\\n* PMCID: PMC11381518\\n* DOI: 10.1038/s41467-024-52149-0\\n  Item in Clipboard\\nHuman cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder\\nContributed equally.\\nTimothy F Shay et al. Nat Commun . 2024 .\\nShow details\\nDisplay options\\nDisplay options\\nFormat Abstract PubMed PMID\\nNat Commun\\nActions\\n* Search in PubMed\\n* Search in NLM Catalog\\n* Add to Search\\n  . 2024 Sep 8;15(1):7853.\\n  doi: 10.1038/s41467-024-52149-0.\\nAffiliations\\nContributed equally.\\n* PMID: 39245720\\n* PMCID: PMC11381518\\n* DOI: 10.1038/s41467-024-52149-0\\n  Item in Clipboard\\n  Full text links Cite\\n  Display options\\n  Display options\\n  Format Abstract PubMed PMID\\nAbstract\\nContributed equally.\\nb SPR confirms serotype-specific interaction of AAV9 with the human immunomodulatory cytokine IL3. c SPR confirms AAV9 binding with macaque but not marmoset or mouse IL3. d SPR confirms that