    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_9ab493d802b835bcdd47a221bd0e0a32\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10115422\",\n      \"title\": \"Primate-conserved carbonic anhydrase IV and murine-restricted LY6C1 enable blood-brain barrier crossing by engineered viral vectors\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2023-04-19\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The APPRAISE-AAV in silico method is well suited to these screens and can be readily applied to any existing engineered capsid library dataset to mine for capsid variants likely to interact with a chosen target receptor, including CA-IV.\\nCoverslips were washed three times in 1× TBS and then incubated with secondary antibody (LY6A and LY6C1, 1:1000 dilution; Invitrogen, A-21247; CA-IV, 1:1000 dilution; Invitrogen, A-21432) in the same medium for 60 min. Coverslips were mounted on slides with Diamond antifade mounting media with 4′,6-diamidino-2-phenylindole (Invitrogen, P36931).\\nWe chose alphafold-multimer-v2 as the default AlphaFold version unless otherwise specified. Each model was recycled three times, and 10 models were generated from each competition.\\nBloom, C. Seed, B. E. Deverman, G. Di Pasquale, Delivering genes across the blood-brain barrier: LY6A, a novel cellular receptor for AAV-PHP.B capsids. PLOS ONE 14, e0225206 (2019). DOI: 10.1371/journal.pone.0225206 PMID: 31725765 PMCID: PMC6855452\\n35. W. A. Liguore, J. S. Domire, D. Button, Y. Wang, B. D. Dufour, S. Srinivasan, J. L.\\nDuan, L. Wang, G. Feng, Y. Chen, N. Y. Ip, J. Pickel, V. Gradinaru, AAV capsid variants with brain-wide transgene expression and decreased liver targeting after intravenous delivery in mouse and marmoset. Nat. Neurosci. 25, 106–115 (2022). DOI: 10.1038/s41593-021-00969-4 PMID: 34887588\\n38. R. J. Samulski, N.\\nShen, X. Ding, T. F. Miles, V. Gradinaru, Structural basis of receptor usage by the engineered capsid AAV-PHP.eB. Mol. Ther. Methods Clin. Dev. 26, 343–354 (2022). DOI: 10.1016/j.omtm.2022.07.011 PMID: 36034770 PMCID: PMC9382559\\n59. P.-S. Huang, Y.-E. A. Ban, F. Richter, I. Andre, R. Vernon, W. R. Schief, D.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13101293\",\n      \"title\": \"Computationally guided discovery of Ly6e/LY6E-dependent AAV capsid variants\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2026-04-01\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Mechanism-guided capsid engineering has recently advanced through approaches that explicitly design capsid-receptor interactions to enable BBB crossing, providing vectors with defined entry mechanisms and predictable tropisms.^31,^32 These studies further highlight that several prominent mouse-derived BBB-crossing capsids depend on LY6A or LY6C1,\\nMethod details\\nEvolutionary optimization combined with APPRAISE (EvoPRAISE) for AAVs\\nThe extracellular domains were determined as homologous sequences based on alignment with the AA27-110 peptide of mouse Ly6A (P05533 · LY6A_MOUSE), which was used in the demonstration of the APPRAISE method.\\nThe publicly available version of APPRAISE was used (available at https://github.com/GradinaruLab/APPRAISE).\\nUnless otherwise specified, the AlphaFold model version alphafold-multimer-v2 was used as the default setting. Each model was recycled 3 times, and 5 models were generated per competition.\\nDeverman B.E., Pravdo P.L., Simpson B.P., Kumar S.R., Chan K.Y., Banerjee A., Wu W.-L., Yang B., Huber N., Pasca S.P., Gradinaru V. Cre-dependent selection yields AAV variants for widespread gene transfer to the adult brain. Nat. Biotechnol. 2016. 34. 204–209. DOI: 10.1038/nbt.3440 PMID: 26829320 PMCID: PMC5088052\\n11.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.adg6618\",\n      \"title\": \"Primate-conserved carbonic anhydrase IV and murine-restricted LY6C1 enable blood-brain barrier crossing by engineered viral vectors | Science Advances\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2023-04-01\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Timothy F. Shay https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6591-3271 , Erin E.\\nLam https://orcid.org/0009-0008-6982-1452 , and Viviana Gradinaru https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5868-348X +10 authors +8 authors +3 authors fewer Authors Info & Affiliations\\nScience Advances\\n19 Apr 2023\\nVol 9 , Issue 16\\nDOI: 10.1126/sciadv.adg6618\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE BBB opening with focused ultrasound in nonhuman primates and Parkinson’s disease patients: Targeted AAV vector delivery and PET imaging Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE A snapshot of the long-term evolution of a distributed tectonic plate boundary Next\\nLY6A is the receptor responsible for the enhanced CNS tropism of PHP.B and\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE BBB opening with focused ultrasound in nonhuman primates and Parkinson’s disease patients: Targeted AAV vector delivery and PET imaging Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE A snapshot of the long-term evolution of a distributed tectonic plate boundary Next\\nred circles, and three hits are marked with red crosses.\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE BBB opening with focused ultrasound in nonhuman primates and Parkinson’s disease patients: Targeted AAV vector delivery and PET imaging Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE A snapshot of the long-term evolution of a distributed tectonic plate boundary Next\\nCoverslips were washed three times in 1× TBS and then incubated with\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE BBB opening with focused ultrasound in nonhuman primates and Parkinson’s disease patients: Targeted AAV vector delivery and PET imaging Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE A snapshot of the long-term evolution of a distributed tectonic plate boundary Next\\nEach model was recycled three times, and 10 models were generated from\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE BBB opening with focused ultrasound in nonhuman primates and Parkinson’s disease patients: Targeted AAV vector delivery and PET imaging Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE A snapshot of the long-term evolution of a distributed tectonic plate boundary Next\\nTissues were then washed one to three times with wash buffer [0.1% Triton\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE BBB opening with focused ultrasound in nonhuman primates and Parkinson’s disease patients: Targeted AAV vector delivery and PET imaging Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE A snapshot of the long-term evolution of a distributed tectonic plate boundary Next\\nfurther washed three times.\\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE BBB opening with focused ultrasound in nonhuman primates and Parkinson’s disease patients: Targeted AAV vector delivery and PET imaging Previous ###### NEXT ARTICLE A snapshot of the long-term evolution of a distributed tectonic plate boundary Next\\nentire Gradinaru l\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2023.01.12.523632v1\",\n      \"title\": \"Primate-conserved Carbonic Anhydrase IV and murine-restricted Ly6c1 are new targets for crossing the blood-brain barrier\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2023-01-13\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"This experimental result further bolsters confidence in in silico APPRAISE-AAV rankings.\\nTo gain a full picture of the AAV-receptor interaction, we next modeled the PHP.eB insertion peptide and Ly6a receptor complex within the context of the AAV capsid three-fold symmetry spike.\\nCoverslips were washed three times in 1x TBS then incubated with secondary (Ly6a & Ly6c1: Invitrogen A-21247, 1:1000 dilution; Car4: Invitrogen A-21432, 1:1000 dilution) in the same medium for 60 minutes. Coverslips were mounted on slides with Diamond Antifade Mounting Media with DAPI (Invitrogen P36931).\\nAutomated Pairwise Peptide Receptor Analysis for Screening Engineered AAVs (APPRAISE-AAV)\\nWe chose alphafold2-multimer-v2 as the default AlphaFold version unless otherwise specified. Each model was recycled three times, and ten models were generated from each competition.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cell.com/cell-reports/pdf/S2211-1247(25)01190-8.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"AAVs targeting human carbonic anhydrase IV enhance gene ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"humanized blood-brain barrier in vivo\\nAuthors\\nChangfan Lin, Xinhong Chen,\\nJonathan D. Hoang, ..., Andrew D. Steele,\\nTimothy F. Shay, Viviana Gradinaru\\nCorrespondence\\nviviana@caltech.edu\\nIn brief\\nAdeno-associated viral vectors (AAVs)\\nbased on natural serotypes poorly\\ntransduce the brain after intravenous\\nadministration, limiting clinical efficacy.\\nAAVs targeted to human CA-IV.\\nRESULTS\\nIn vitro selections successfully identify known receptor-\\nAAV pairs\\nThe process of receptor-mediated BBB crossing by AAVs in-\\nvolves three key steps: receptor binding, cellular uptake, and\\ntranscytosis ( Figure 1 B). 31 , 44–46 First, AAVs circulating in the\\n18 , 31–33 , 47 Leveraging this\\nunderstanding, we designed a receptor-directed in vitro engi-\\nneering workflow to identify AAV capsid variants that effec-\\ntively interact with validated BBB transcytosis receptors.\\nOur approach builds upon recent successes targeting other\\nBBB receptors (such as murine LY6A and widely expressed\\nhuman TfR1). 31 , 43\\nreceptor by calculating its enhancement (Eh, Equation 2 ):\\n(D) Pull-down-based selection workflow used to identify AAV variants that bind target receptors. Capsid variants that bind to the receptor (e.g., CA-IV or LY6A) are\\nisolated with receptor-bound beads, followed by DNA extraction and NGS to identify the enriched variants.\\nHaving validated our method with known receptor-AAV pairs, we\\nnext turned to the identification of capsid variants that engage\\npreviously untargeted receptors, specifically human CA-IV.\\nFollowing the same approach used above for LY6A, mouse\\nCA-IV, and previously, 31 , 43 we conducted one round of selection\\nDeverman, B.E., Pravdo, P.L., Simpson, B.P., Kumar, S.R., Chan, K.Y.,\\nBanerjee, A., Wu, W.L., Yang, B., Huber, N., Pasca, S.P., and Gradinaru,\\nV. (2016). Cre-dependent selection yields AAV variants for widespread\\ngene transfer to the adult brain. Nat. Biotechnol. 34 , 204–209. https://\\ndoi.org/10.1038/nbt.3440 .\\n18.\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1101/2024.01.05.574399 .\\n35. Huang, Q., Chan, K.Y., Tobey, I.G., Chan, Y.A., Poterba, T., Boutros, C.L.,\\nBalazs, A.B., Daneman, R., Bloom, J.M., Seed, C., and Deverman, B.E.\\n(2019). Delivering genes across the blood-brain barrier: LY6A, a novel\\ncellular receptor for AAV-PHP.B capsids. PLoS One 14 , e0225206.\\nChan, K.Y., Jang, M.J., Yoo, B.B., Greenbaum, A., Ravi, N., Wu, W.L.,\\nSa´ nchez-Guardado, L., Lois, C., Mazmanian, S.K., Deverman, B.E., and\\nGradinaru, V. (2017). Engineered AAVs for efficient noninvasive gene deliv-\\nery to the central and peripheral nervous systems. Nat. Neurosci. 20 ,\\n1172–1179. https://doi.org/10.1038/nn.4593 .\\n49.\\nJang, S., Shen, H.K., Ding, X., Miles, T.F., and Gradinaru, V. (2022). Struc-\\ntural basis of receptor usage by the engineered capsid AAV-PHP.eB. Mol.\\nTher. Methods Clin. Dev. 26 ,\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37075114\",\n      \"title\": \"Primate-conserved carbonic anhydrase IV and murine-restricted LY6C1 enable blood-brain barrier crossing by engineered viral vectors - PubMed\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2023-04-21\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Timothy F Shay 1 , Erin E Sullivan 1 , Xiaozhe Ding 1 , Xinhong Chen 1 , Sripriya Ravindra Kumar 1 , David Goertsen 1 , David Brown 1 , Anaya Crosby 2 , Jost Vielmetter 1 , Máté Borsos 1 , Damien A Wolfe 1 , Annie W Lam 1 , Viviana Gradinaru 1\\nAffiliations Expand\\nAffiliations\\nWe apply AlphaFold-based in silico methods to generate capsid-receptor binding models to predict the affinity of AAVs for these identified receptors.\\nIn silico ranking of AAV variants by their receptor binding propensities.\\n( A …\\nFig. 5.. In silico ranking of AAV variants by their receptor binding propensities.\\n( A ) Overview of AlphaFold-based in silico Automated Pairwise Peptide-Receptor Analysis for Screening Engineered AAVs (APPRAISE-AAV for short) ( 45 ).\\nCompetition results reflect the relative peptide binding probability encoded in the AlphaFold neural network. ( B ) Table of engineered AAV capsids, their confirmed receptor, and the capsid peptide sequence used in APPRAISE-AAV. References: 1: Gao et al. ( 27 ), 2: Deverman et al. ( 17 ), 3: Chan et al. ( 26 ), 4: Nonnenmacher et al.\\n( C ) Complete model of the PHP.eB trimer and LY6A complex. The AlphaFold-Multimer structural prediction from (A) was combined with a capsid monomer-receptor structural prediction and optimized using Rosetta Remodel within the context of the AAV trimer (fig. S6A).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8092683\",\n      \"title\": \"Adeno-associated Virus (AAV) Capsid Chimeras with Enhanced Infectivity Reveal a Core Element in the AAV Genome Critical for both Cell Transduction and Capsid Assembly\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2021-03-10\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Comparison of the viral titer of the purified wild-type AAV6 and these three capsid variants by quantitative PCR (qPCR) after DNase I treatment showed significantly lower titers, by 2 to 3 log, for the three variants (Fig. 5B).\\nAs expected, abundant levels of the three AAV6 capsid proteins could be readily detected in the cell lysates, whereas very low to undetectable levels of VP1 to VP3 were observed with both variants 5 and 5.3 in the absence of cotransfected AAP (Fig. 7A).\\nHowever, when the capsid proteins were analyzed from purified vectors, all three VP proteins were detectable at the expected ratio of approximately 1:1:10 for wild-type AAV6 and variants 5 and 5.3 (Fig. 7B).\\nThus, the observed enhanced efficiency of infection is likely to be a postentry effect due to improved interaction with trafficking receptors/effectors.\\nThe transfected cells were harvested 72 h posttransfection, pelleted, and subjected to three freeze-thaw cycles to release the AAV vectors from the cells.\\nFor the analysis of VP expression of the different capsid variants, HEK293 cells were transfected as described above, with and without the addition of AAP, and cells were lysed 72 h posttransfection by three cycles of freezing and thawing.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0225206\",\n      \"title\": \"Delivering genes across the blood-brain barrier: LY6A, a novel cellular receptor for AAV-PHP.B capsids | PLOS One\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"To guide their application across disease models, and to inspire the development of translational gene therapy vectors for targeting neurological diseases in humans, we sought to elucidate the host factors responsible for the CNS tropism of the AAV-PHP.B vectors. Leveraging CNS tropism differences across 13 mouse strains, we systematically determined a set of genetic variants that segregate with the permissivity phenotype, and rapidly identified LY6A as an essential receptor for the AAV-PHP.B vectors. Interfering with LY6A by CRISPR/Cas9-mediated _Ly6a_ disruption or with blocking antibodies reduced transduction of mouse brain endothelial cells by AAV-PHP.eB, while ectopic expression of _Ly6a_ increased AAV-PHP.eB transduction of HEK293T and CHO cells by 30-fold or more.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36034770\",\n      \"title\": \"Structural basis of receptor usage by the engineered capsid AAV-PHP.eB.\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2022-07-31\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Structural basis of receptor usage by the engineered capsid AAV-PHP.eB.\\nAuthors: Jang Seongmin, Shen Hao K, Ding Xiaozhe, Miles Timothy F, Gradinaru Viviana\\nJournal: Molecular therapy. Methods & clinical development. 2022;26:343-354.\\nPublished: 2022-07-31\\nPublication types: Journal Article\\nPMID: 36034770; DOI: 10.1016/j.omtm.2022.07.011; PMCID: PMC9382559\\nAbstract\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.broadinstitute.org/publications/broad625921\",\n      \"title\": \"Delivering genes across the blood-brain barrier: LY6A, a novel cellular receptor for AAV-PHP.B capsids. | Broad Institute\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Breadcrumb\\n1. Home\\n2. Publications\\nDelivering genes across the blood-brain barrier: LY6A, a novel cellular receptor for AAV-PHP.B capsids.\\nPLoS One\\n|Authors |Qin Huang\\nKen Chan\\nIsabelle Tobey\\nYujia Chan\\nTim Poterba\\nChristine Boutros\\nAlejandro Balazs\\nRichard Daneman\\nJonathan Bloom\\nCotton Seed\\nLeveraging CNS tropism differences across 13 mouse strains, we systematically determined a set of genetic variants that segregate with the permissivity phenotype, and rapidly identified LY6A as an essential receptor for the AAV-PHP.B vectors.\\nInterfering with LY6A by CRISPR/Cas9-mediated Ly6a disruption or with blocking antibodies reduced transduction of mouse brain endothelial cells by AAV-PHP.eB, while ectopic expression of Ly6a increased AAV-PHP.eB transduction of HEK293T and CHO cells by 30-fold or more.\\nImportantly, we demonstrate that this newly discovered mode of AAV binding and transduction can occur independently of other known AAV receptors.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_ea39878bfffeVgc2zisEYeoaLE\"\n}"
